@[init-system step]
The init system starts and stops everything else. The base system you just
installed uses @[simpleinit-msb] with SMGL's init.d scripts, Source Mage's
classic setup. If that is what you want, there is nothing to do here: type
@[next]. Otherwise (both need network, see @[guide network]):

@[systemd] (journald, logind, unit files, systemd-boot):
  @[enchantment install init systemd]
  sorcery compiles systemd, linux-pam, polkit and a few libraries, and
  recompiles the spells that gain systemd or PAM support (util-linux,
  e2fsprogs, shadow, openssh, ...): 20 minutes to a few hours. It replaces
  simpleinit-msb and eudev and writes a PAM setup (/etc/pam.d/system-auth).
  Afterwards: @[todo systemd], and @[todo systemd-boot] for its boot loader.

@[OpenRC] (Gentoo's and Alpine's init: shell scripts with dependencies):
  @[enchantment install init openrc]
  sorcery compiles openrc and libcap and recompiles procps-ng: a few
  minutes. It replaces simpleinit-msb and init.d, installs service scripts
  (udev, metalog, nscd, sshd, networking for /etc/network/interfaces,
  dhcpcd, wpa_supplicant) and carries the clock, keymap and font settings
  over. Afterwards: @[todo openrc].

Each cast ends with the spells' notices, also kept in
$ENCHANT_TARGET/tmp/cast/backup/*/notice_log (read them before rebooting).
sorcery normally compiles on one CPU core (MAKE_NJOBS); unless you set it,
the command uses all cores while it runs and then puts the setting back. To
set it for good (leaving 2 or 3 threads free keeps a machine usable):
@[enchantment chroot], @[sorcery], Option Menu, Parallel/Cached/Distributed
Compiling, EXTRA_JOBS. If the new init can't be installed (network, a failed
build), the command puts simpleinit-msb back, so the system still boots.

You can also switch later, from the installed system, by casting the init
yourself; the installer's command does the extra work for you.

When you are ready, type @[next].
